1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,050
Pulau Semakau: From Fishing Village
to the Island Made of Rubbish

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:09,407
In 1977, Minah Bte Gap and
her husband Rani Bin Omar

3
00:00:09,487 --> 00:00:14,014
were resettled from Pulau
Semakau to a one-room rental

4
00:00:14,094 --> 00:00:18,275
flat at Telok Blangah, on
the Singapore mainland.

5
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:20,550
They rarely slept in it.

6
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:24,372
They gave the flat to their
son, went back across the water,

7
00:00:24,452 --> 00:00:28,925
and lived on in a run-down beach hut
on the island they had been moved

8
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:32,957
out of — fishing, selling the
good catches to the islanders of

9
00:00:33,037 --> 00:00:37,525
neighbouring Pulau Seking, cooking
fish and rice on a kerosene stove.

10
00:00:37,525 --> 00:00:40,478
Minah was ninety when the makers of the

11
00:00:40,558 --> 00:00:43,825
Island Nation documentary found the story.

12
00:00:43,825 --> 00:00:47,275
"Our hearts are attached
to the island," she said.

13
00:00:47,275 --> 00:00:51,255
"We can't do the same things on
Singapore, no?" The island she

14
00:00:51,335 --> 00:00:55,903
could not leave is now Singapore's
landfill — the only one it has left,

15
00:00:55,983 --> 00:00:59,650
an island quite literally
made of the country's rubbish.

16
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:03,475
This is the story of the two
fishing villages that stood where

17
00:01:03,555 --> 00:01:07,633
the rubbish goes, of the strange
living island the landfill turned

18
00:01:07,712 --> 00:01:11,664
out to be, and of the newest turn
in its life: the effort to dig

19
00:01:11,744 --> 00:01:15,775
the rubbish back up, in a manner
of speaking, and build with it.

20
00:01:15,775 --> 00:01:19,195
Pulau Semakau lies about
eight kilometres south

21
00:01:19,275 --> 00:01:22,775
of the main island, among
the southern islands.

22
00:01:22,775 --> 00:01:27,715
Its name is thought to come from
bakau, the mangrove: in 1844 it

23
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:32,579
was recorded as Mangrove Island,
home to about a dozen Chinese

24
00:01:32,659 --> 00:01:38,150
smallholders growing vegetables,
raising poultry and burning charcoal.

25
00:01:38,150 --> 00:01:41,601
More settlers came in the
early twentieth century,

26
00:01:41,681 --> 00:01:45,767
principally Teochew Chinese,
and the village that grew up —

27
00:01:45,847 --> 00:01:49,086
Kampong Tengah, some 160
houses on the island's

28
00:01:49,166 --> 00:01:53,050
western side — was a mixed
Chinese and Malay community.

29
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:56,652
By 1955 it held about
780 people, who farmed,

30
00:01:56,732 --> 00:02:01,725
kept goats and poultry, and
fished from small kolek boats with

31
00:02:01,805 --> 00:02:06,550
traps and nets: bream, mullet,
grouper, snapper, stingray.

32
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:10,800
Most of the catch was sold
to middlemen on the mainland.

33
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:14,650
It was a self-contained
world, and a hard one.

34
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:16,581
The wells were unreliable,

35
00:02:16,661 --> 00:02:20,450
so fresh water was ferried
over from Pulau Bukom.

36
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:25,300
Electricity came only in the
evenings, when the generator ran.

37
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:29,975
There were no sewers; the communal
latrines stood over the water.

38
00:02:29,975 --> 00:02:34,844
Medical care arrived once a week,
in the person of a nurse who came

39
00:02:34,924 --> 00:02:39,800
by boat from Pulau Brani and was
known as the Floating Dispensary.

40
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,900
But the village also had a
mosque, a community centre,

41
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:47,847
a football field, a police
post and burial grounds,

42
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:51,875
and from 1951 a primary
school with seven teachers.

43
00:02:51,875 --> 00:02:55,584
One of those seven was a
young man named Sidek Saniff —

44
00:02:55,664 --> 00:02:59,373
who would grow up to become
Singapore's Senior Minister

45
00:02:59,453 --> 00:03:02,887
of State for the Environment,
in the ministry whose

46
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:06,825
agency now runs the landfill
on his old school's island.

47
00:03:06,825 --> 00:03:10,536
In the late 1960s the
village took in neighbours:

48
00:03:10,616 --> 00:03:16,650
families resettled from Pulau Bukom as
the petrochemical works there expanded.

49
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:20,172
The southern islands' people
were being shuffled from

50
00:03:20,252 --> 00:03:24,125
island to island long before
anyone mentioned a landfill.

51
00:03:24,125 --> 00:03:27,827
Ten minutes away by sampan
lay the second island,

52
00:03:27,907 --> 00:03:29,450
and the older world.

53
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:32,812
Pulau Seking — also
spelled Sakeng — was tiny,

54
00:03:32,892 --> 00:03:35,880
about five hundred metres by two hundred,

55
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:40,600
its houses on pillars over the
water around most of its coast.

56
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:45,202
Its people considered themselves
Orang Selat, "people of the Straits",

57
00:03:45,282 --> 00:03:48,547
one of the peoples
collectively called Orang Laut,

58
00:03:48,627 --> 00:03:52,975
the sea people who were the original
inhabitants of these shores.

59
00:03:52,975 --> 00:03:56,503
They had lived on Seking
since before colonial times;

60
00:03:56,583 --> 00:04:00,723
finds on the island date to the
fourteenth century or earlier,

61
00:04:00,803 --> 00:04:05,420
and anthropologists who worked there
in the late 1970s called it "the

62
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:09,504
final link to pre-Raffles
Singapore", its families still kin

63
00:04:09,584 --> 00:04:13,600
to the islanders of Pulau
Pangkil across the water in Riau.

64
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:19,049
In 1957 the census counted 217 people;

65
00:04:19,129 --> 00:04:22,475
by 1980 there were 525.

66
00:04:22,475 --> 00:04:26,011
They fished and built boats,
as their ancestors had,

67
00:04:26,091 --> 00:04:29,418
and as Pulau Bukom grew
into an oil refinery many

68
00:04:29,498 --> 00:04:32,975
of them crossed over to
work there as technicians.

69
00:04:32,975 --> 00:04:36,549
The island had its mosque,
its schools, its clinic,

70
00:04:36,629 --> 00:04:40,419
its police post, and a
provision shop run from a stilt

71
00:04:40,499 --> 00:04:44,225
house by two brothers, Teo
Yen Eng and Teo Yen Teck.

72
00:04:44,225 --> 00:04:48,233
Island lore said the place took
its name from Keng, a woman of

73
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:51,860
Riau blood who arrived more
than two hundred years ago,

74
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:57,069
healed the sick, defended the island
against pirates, and became its chieftain;

75
00:04:57,149 --> 00:05:00,775
her shrine stood on the hill
until the bulldozers came.

76
00:05:00,775 --> 00:05:04,482
The southern islands also
took turns hosting a year-end

77
00:05:04,562 --> 00:05:09,234
carnival with the Riau islanders —
swimming, diving and rowing races,

78
00:05:09,314 --> 00:05:11,713
a greasy-pole contest, sepak takraw,

79
00:05:11,793 --> 00:05:16,326
top-spinning — a whole sea-going
society's calendar, which faded as

80
00:05:16,406 --> 00:05:20,400
the visits grew harder and the
islands emptied one by one.

81
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:22,677
The villages did not end together,

82
00:05:22,757 --> 00:05:25,600
and they did not end for the same reason.

83
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:29,937
Kampong Tengah was relocated
to the mainland in 1977,

84
00:05:30,017 --> 00:05:33,270
almost twenty years before the landfill,

85
00:05:33,350 --> 00:05:37,350
for reasons of the islands'
general development.

86
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:40,275
Some went quietly; some did not.

87
00:05:40,275 --> 00:05:44,795
Mak Imah, ordered to resettle,
dismantled her house on Semakau,

88
00:05:44,875 --> 00:05:47,935
carried it across to
Pulau Pemping in Riau,

89
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:52,250
and rebuilt it there, rather
than move to a mainland flat.

90
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:57,150
Minah and Rani took the flat and
then, in effect, declined it.

91
00:05:57,150 --> 00:06:00,869
The island was left to nature
for about twenty years;

92
00:06:00,949 --> 00:06:03,879
along its western shore you can still find

93
00:06:03,959 --> 00:06:07,400
the eroded piles and
foundations of the village.

94
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:10,066
Seking's goodbye was longer and harder,

95
00:06:10,146 --> 00:06:14,361
because Seking's people had
further to fall — from the oldest

96
00:06:14,441 --> 00:06:18,525
continuous community in
Singapore's waters to none at all.

97
00:06:18,525 --> 00:06:23,450
Development was first
announced in June 1975.

98
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:27,916
In 1983 it was announced that
Jurong Town Corporation would take

99
00:06:27,996 --> 00:06:32,178
over the island by the end of
the decade or the early 1990s,

100
00:06:32,258 --> 00:06:36,795
and there were public calls to
save it; the reply was that it was

101
00:06:36,875 --> 00:06:41,350
earmarked for long-term industrial
use, with no immediate plan.

102
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:46,925
By 1993 the population had fallen to 139.

103
00:06:46,925 --> 00:06:51,582
On the 11th of November 1993 the
Minister for the Environment,

104
00:06:51,662 --> 00:06:55,096
Mah Bow Tan, told
Parliament that the landfill

105
00:06:55,176 --> 00:06:59,225
would be built in the sea
between Seking and Semakau.

106
00:06:59,225 --> 00:07:02,820
Kanwaljit Soin, a Nominated
Member of Parliament,

107
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:07,025
appealed for Seking to be
excluded on heritage grounds.

108
00:07:07,025 --> 00:07:10,501
The appeal was refused:
keeping the little island

109
00:07:10,581 --> 00:07:13,767
out of the scheme would
have cost another 130

110
00:07:13,847 --> 00:07:17,475
million Singapore dollars
for an alternative site.

111
00:07:17,475 --> 00:07:22,538
In March 1994 Berita Harian
ran the headline "Raya terakhir

112
00:07:22,618 --> 00:07:27,325
di Pulau Seking" — the last
Hari Raya on Pulau Seking.

113
00:07:27,325 --> 00:07:31,144
That year the remaining hundred
and thirty or so residents

114
00:07:31,224 --> 00:07:35,445
left for Housing Board flats on
the mainland, and the community,

115
00:07:35,525 --> 00:07:38,550
in the words of the
record, became dispersed.

116
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:42,085
Saniah Binte Ali, the widow
of Mohamed Yatim Bin Akib,

117
00:07:42,165 --> 00:07:46,839
Seking's last penghulu — its headman
— remembered the exchange plainly:

118
00:07:46,919 --> 00:07:49,918
on the island, she said,
grandmother was free,

119
00:07:49,998 --> 00:07:54,350
could walk here, walk there; in
the flat it was like being caged.

120
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:58,575
Why did Singapore need its
sea for a rubbish tip at all?

121
00:07:58,575 --> 00:08:01,600
Because the mainland had filled up.

122
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:05,055
The Lorong Halus dumping
ground in the north-east,

123
00:08:05,135 --> 00:08:10,215
opened in 1970, had at one point taken
almost half the country's rubbish,

124
00:08:10,295 --> 00:08:12,972
and for years the neighbours complained

125
00:08:13,052 --> 00:08:16,375
of its smell, its open
fires and its pollution.

126
00:08:16,375 --> 00:08:19,123
The Lim Chu Kang ground in the

127
00:08:19,203 --> 00:08:22,125
west filled and closed in 1992.

128
00:08:22,125 --> 00:08:25,512
Lorong Halus itself closed on the

129
00:08:25,592 --> 00:08:29,375
31st of March 1999, completely full.

130
00:08:29,375 --> 00:08:32,975
Semakau Landfill opened the next morning.

131
00:08:32,975 --> 00:08:36,488
Building it took from 1995 to 1999 and,

132
00:08:36,568 --> 00:08:41,003
on the National Environment
Agency's own history,

133
00:08:41,083 --> 00:08:44,400
about 610 million Singapore dollars.

134
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:48,436
The design was simple to
describe and enormous to do:

135
00:08:48,516 --> 00:08:54,725
a seven-kilometre wall of rock — a perimeter
bund — drawn through the sea between

136
00:08:54,805 --> 00:08:59,775
Pulau Semakau and Pulau Seking,
enclosing 350 hectares of water.

137
00:08:59,775 --> 00:09:04,778
The bund is lined with an impermeable
membrane and a layer of marine clay,

138
00:09:04,858 --> 00:09:08,487
so that the liquid that
drains from buried waste stays

139
00:09:08,567 --> 00:09:13,100
inside and is treated on site
rather than leaking into the strait.

140
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,900
The enclosed sea was divided into cells.

141
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:19,678
And on top of little Pulau Seking itself —

142
00:09:19,758 --> 00:09:23,692
the island of the Orang Selat,
of Keng's shrine and the Teo

143
00:09:23,772 --> 00:09:28,455
brothers' shop — the engineers built
the landfill's receiving station,

144
00:09:28,535 --> 00:09:31,325
the cavernous hall where the barges dock.

145
00:09:31,325 --> 00:09:34,975
The village island is
still there, in a sense.

146
00:09:34,975 --> 00:09:37,750
It is the front door of the tip.

147
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:40,661
Almost nothing that Singapore throws

148
00:09:40,741 --> 00:09:43,400
away goes to Semakau as rubbish.

149
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:47,685
The country's waste goes first
to its waste-to-energy plants,

150
00:09:47,765 --> 00:09:51,700
where burning cuts its volume
by about ninety per cent.

151
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:55,521
What cannot be burned, and
what the burning leaves behind,

152
00:09:55,601 --> 00:09:57,350
is what makes the journey.

153
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:01,106
Residues are trucked to the
Tuas Marine Transfer Station

154
00:10:01,186 --> 00:10:04,121
on the mainland, loaded
into covered barges,

155
00:10:04,201 --> 00:10:08,299
and pushed by tugs on a 33-kilometre
sea journey to Semakau —

156
00:10:08,379 --> 00:10:12,615
the barges travel at night, and
the whole operation is arranged

157
00:10:12,695 --> 00:10:16,600
so that the island takes
delivery while Singapore sleeps.

158
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:20,762
At the receiving station,
excavators with specially designed

159
00:10:20,842 --> 00:10:24,580
grabs lift the ash out of
the barges and into 35-tonne

160
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:28,397
off-road dump trucks, which
run along a ten-metre-wide

161
00:10:28,477 --> 00:10:32,074
road on top of the bund to
whichever cell is active,

162
00:10:32,154 --> 00:10:36,325
where bulldozers and compactors
level and compact the fill.

163
00:10:36,325 --> 00:10:40,905
On the agency's 2019
figures the island receives

164
00:10:40,985 --> 00:10:45,177
about 2,100 tonnes a
day — some 1,500 tonnes

165
00:10:45,257 --> 00:10:51,002
of incineration ash and 600
tonnes of non-incinerable waste,

166
00:10:51,082 --> 00:10:53,800
about 700,000 tonnes a year.

167
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:58,035
Before the ash is even barged,
a plant at Tuas run by REMEX,

168
00:10:58,115 --> 00:11:01,272
operating since 2015,
pulls tens of thousands

169
00:11:01,352 --> 00:11:06,306
of tonnes of metal out of it with
magnets and eddy-current separators,

170
00:11:06,386 --> 00:11:08,975
cutting its weight by about a tenth.

171
00:11:08,975 --> 00:11:12,925
When a cell is full it is
covered with earth — and then,

172
00:11:13,005 --> 00:11:17,250
disconcertingly for anyone
expecting a tip, it turns green.

173
00:11:17,250 --> 00:11:20,388
Grass and trees take
root over the buried ash,

174
00:11:20,468 --> 00:11:24,525
and a filled cell becomes a
meadow with a view of the sea.

175
00:11:24,525 --> 00:11:28,575
The surprise of Semakau — the
thing that has drawn journalists

176
00:11:28,655 --> 00:11:32,373
to call it a "Garbage of Eden"
— is that the landfill was

177
00:11:32,453 --> 00:11:36,450
built against a living shore,
and the shore is still living.

178
00:11:36,450 --> 00:11:40,117
The landfill was anchored
to Semakau's eastern side;

179
00:11:40,197 --> 00:11:43,143
the western shoreline, with its mangroves,

180
00:11:43,223 --> 00:11:46,825
seagrass meadows and reef
flats, was left natural.

181
00:11:46,825 --> 00:11:51,656
Where construction did take nature
away, some of it was put back:

182
00:11:51,736 --> 00:11:55,283
building the bund cost
13.6 hectares of mangrove

183
00:11:55,363 --> 00:11:59,514
on the eastern shore, and the
agency replanted two plots

184
00:11:59,594 --> 00:12:03,750
totalling the same 13.6
hectares just outside the bund.

185
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:08,800
The replanted mangroves thrive —
and that is not just decoration.

186
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:12,737
Healthy mangroves growing hard
against the bund are taken

187
00:12:12,817 --> 00:12:16,975
as living evidence that the
liners beneath are not leaking.

188
00:12:16,975 --> 00:12:20,925
The landfill's first
alarm system is a forest.

189
00:12:20,925 --> 00:12:24,408
At low tide the western
flats are a roll-call of

190
00:12:24,488 --> 00:12:28,200
things that are rare
everywhere else in Singapore.

191
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:31,807
The island holds the country's
largest visitable bed

192
00:12:31,887 --> 00:12:35,849
of tape seagrass, stretching
for kilometres, and the only

193
00:12:35,929 --> 00:12:38,544
known local stand of another seagrass,

194
00:12:38,624 --> 00:12:43,375
Syringodium isoetifolium, found
nowhere else in Singapore's waters.

195
00:12:43,375 --> 00:12:45,925
Knobbly sea stars — large, endangered,

196
00:12:46,005 --> 00:12:49,950
and in good numbers here —
sit out the tide on the flats.

197
00:12:49,950 --> 00:12:53,948
Giant clams, including the
endangered fluted giant clam,

198
00:12:54,028 --> 00:12:58,245
live on the reef; so do seahorses,
turtles and reef sharks,

199
00:12:58,325 --> 00:13:01,675
with dolphins and otters
in the waters around.

200
00:13:01,675 --> 00:13:06,562
A survey in 2005 turned up four
plants endangered in Singapore,

201
00:13:06,642 --> 00:13:10,900
one of them then recorded
nowhere else in the country.

202
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:13,720
The birds include the great-billed heron —

203
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:16,759
one long-term resident
was nicknamed Jimmy —

204
00:13:16,839 --> 00:13:20,775
Malaysian plovers, brahminy
kites and mangrove whistlers.

205
00:13:20,775 --> 00:13:23,883
Since 2007 a team of
volunteers, TeamSeagrass,

206
00:13:23,963 --> 00:13:29,010
has monitored the meadow, and that
same year a coral nursery was set up in

207
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:32,822
the sea beside the island —
the region's first to raise

208
00:13:32,902 --> 00:13:37,395
"corals of opportunity", fragments
already broken off by accident,

209
00:13:37,475 --> 00:13:41,425
to be grown on and transplanted
to tired reefs elsewhere.

210
00:13:41,425 --> 00:13:46,425
The strangest chapter of the
living island came in 2014.

211
00:13:46,425 --> 00:13:50,614
The landfill's second phase
required closing the gap in the bund

212
00:13:50,694 --> 00:13:54,951
and filling the southern lagoon
— a lagoon that, having been left

213
00:13:55,031 --> 00:13:59,100
open to the sea for sixteen
years, had quietly become a reef.

214
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:03,600
Before it could be filled, it had
to be emptied of its wildlife.

215
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:08,264
A survey team inventoried it;
divers then harvested more than

216
00:14:08,344 --> 00:14:12,229
seven hundred coral colonies
and transplanted them,

217
00:14:12,309 --> 00:14:17,675
over the winter of 2014 to 2015, to
the Sisters' Islands Marine Park.

218
00:14:17,675 --> 00:14:22,206
Among them were rarities: a
fluted giant clam, and a Neptune's

219
00:14:22,286 --> 00:14:26,148
Cup sponge — a species
recorded in Singapore in 1908,

220
00:14:26,228 --> 00:14:31,056
long thought extinct, rediscovered
at Saint John's Island in 2011,

221
00:14:31,136 --> 00:14:35,450
and found again, improbably,
growing in a landfill lagoon.

222
00:14:35,450 --> 00:14:39,056
Nature groups then caught
and released more than

223
00:14:39,136 --> 00:14:42,975
450 fish from the lagoon
before the filling began.

224
00:14:42,975 --> 00:14:45,782
They had to rescue a reef from the rubbish

225
00:14:45,863 --> 00:14:49,025
island's own lagoon
before they could bury it.

226
00:14:49,025 --> 00:14:53,175
For a time, Singaporeans
could see all this themselves.

227
00:14:53,175 --> 00:14:57,320
On the 16th of July 2005 the
agency opened the landfill to the

228
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:00,660
public for guided intertidal
walks, birdwatching,

229
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:05,840
catch-and-release fishing and stargazing
— and among the guests of honour at

230
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:10,270
the opening were forty families
of former Pulau Seking residents,

231
00:15:10,350 --> 00:15:14,575
invited back as guests to the
island that had been their home.

232
00:15:14,575 --> 00:15:19,163
The Teo brothers, the provision-shop
keepers, had made their own

233
00:15:19,243 --> 00:15:24,275
return the year before, in 2004, to
see what their island had become.

234
00:15:24,275 --> 00:15:27,494
Visits ran for years,
thousands of people came,

235
00:15:27,574 --> 00:15:31,225
and the walks were a fixture
of the nature calendar.

236
00:15:31,225 --> 00:15:35,063
They are, for now, suspended
— the agency's page currently

237
00:15:35,143 --> 00:15:38,644
offers visits to its
waste-to-energy plants instead —

238
00:15:38,724 --> 00:15:43,440
so the island has gone back to being
what it was for most Singaporeans:

239
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:46,425
a name, a rumour, a
place the rubbish goes.

240
00:15:46,425 --> 00:15:51,301
When Semakau was designed, it
was meant to last until 2045 —

241
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:54,850
nearly half a century of the nation's ash.

242
00:15:54,850 --> 00:15:59,838
That date has been walking backwards
ever since: coverage in the

243
00:15:59,918 --> 00:16:05,460
mid-2000s said 2040; when the second
phase was completed in July 2015 —

244
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:10,765
closing the lagoon into a single
giant cell and adding 16.7 million

245
00:16:10,845 --> 00:16:16,150
cubic metres of room — the agency's
own line was "2035 and beyond".

246
00:16:16,150 --> 00:16:19,854
At current filling rates,
the expectation now

247
00:16:19,934 --> 00:16:23,550
is that Semakau will
be full by about 2035.

248
00:16:23,550 --> 00:16:28,172
Singapore's Zero Waste Masterplan
sets the task of cutting what is

249
00:16:28,252 --> 00:16:32,588
sent to Semakau by thirty per
cent by 2030, but the arithmetic

250
00:16:32,668 --> 00:16:37,361
underneath is blunt: a city that
burns its rubbish still makes ash,

251
00:16:37,441 --> 00:16:42,000
the ash has to go somewhere, and
there is exactly one somewhere.

252
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,788
Which is why the island's newest chapter

253
00:16:44,868 --> 00:16:47,450
is not about burying the ash better.

254
00:16:47,450 --> 00:16:50,275
It is about not burying it at all.

255
00:16:50,275 --> 00:16:54,300
The idea is older than
the problem's urgency.

256
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:58,559
As far back as 2001, the environment
ministry was studying whether

257
00:16:58,639 --> 00:17:03,555
incineration bottom ash — the heavy
residue left in the grate after burning,

258
00:17:03,635 --> 00:17:07,433
roughly a fifth of what goes
in — could be treated and used

259
00:17:07,513 --> 00:17:11,772
as a building material. (The other
residue, fly ash, is hazardous;

260
00:17:11,852 --> 00:17:16,125
it is stabilised and sealed away,
and is not part of this story.)

261
00:17:16,125 --> 00:17:20,591
In 2009 a road trial using a
stabilisation technique got

262
00:17:20,671 --> 00:17:24,650
treated bottom ash written
into the Land Transport

263
00:17:24,730 --> 00:17:29,600
Authority's roadworks specifications
as a possible material.

264
00:17:29,600 --> 00:17:34,081
The agency gave the material a
brand deliberately echoing NEWater:

265
00:17:34,161 --> 00:17:37,881
NEWSand — treated ash, washed
and chemically stabilised

266
00:17:37,961 --> 00:17:42,028
so that contaminants cannot
leach out, made to environmental

267
00:17:42,108 --> 00:17:46,105
standards the agency wrote for
it, for use in place of sand

268
00:17:46,185 --> 00:17:50,400
and fine aggregate in road bases
and non-structural concrete.

269
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:57,056
A parallel track gasifies waste at up
to 1,600 degrees into a glass-like slag,

270
00:17:57,136 --> 00:18:00,850
which is made into
NEWSand of another kind.

271
00:18:00,850 --> 00:18:04,900
Some of it is already
under Singaporeans' feet.

272
00:18:04,900 --> 00:18:08,849
Slag from the national
waste-to-energy research facility

273
00:18:08,929 --> 00:18:12,734
at Tuas was cast into a
concrete plaza in front of the

274
00:18:12,814 --> 00:18:17,482
Environment Building on Scotts
Road, and into a 105-metre footpath

275
00:18:17,562 --> 00:18:21,375
linking Our Tampines Hub to
the Darul Ghufran Mosque.

276
00:18:21,375 --> 00:18:26,291
The serious test came at Tanah Merah
Coast Road: from September 2020,

277
00:18:26,371 --> 00:18:31,432
about 3,000 tonnes of treated bottom
ash, processed by three companies,

278
00:18:31,512 --> 00:18:35,197
went into the road base and
sub-base of a real road,

279
00:18:35,277 --> 00:18:40,200
with environmental monitoring before,
during and after construction.

280
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:44,183
The road sections opened
to traffic in April 2022,

281
00:18:44,262 --> 00:18:49,300
and the ash has performed
comparably to conventional material.

282
00:18:49,300 --> 00:18:51,554
But the honest status is this:

283
00:18:51,634 --> 00:18:55,525
proven in a real road, not
yet a routine material.

284
00:18:55,525 --> 00:18:59,102
The standards review the
trial feeds is still the gate,

285
00:18:59,182 --> 00:19:03,558
and research now running — including
a university project examining

286
00:19:03,638 --> 00:19:07,015
the ash's behaviour at
fifteen possible reuse sites,

287
00:19:07,095 --> 00:19:11,005
and whether it might one day
serve in structural concrete or

288
00:19:11,085 --> 00:19:15,075
coastal protection — is still
working to finalise the rules.

289
00:19:15,075 --> 00:19:17,580
Behind the trials stands a funding

290
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:20,625
machine built for exactly this problem.

291
00:19:20,625 --> 00:19:24,902
The Closing the Waste Loop
initiative of 2017 put 45 million

292
00:19:24,982 --> 00:19:29,768
Singapore dollars into research on
diverting waste — ash included —

293
00:19:29,848 --> 00:19:34,489
away from the landfill; its
successor, Closing the Resource Loop,

294
00:19:34,569 --> 00:19:38,120
added 80 million Singapore
dollars in 2022 with an

295
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:42,775
emphasis on getting laboratory
results built in the real world.

296
00:19:42,775 --> 00:19:44,575
Under the first programme,

297
00:19:44,655 --> 00:19:51,228
Republic Polytechnic researchers developed a
way to turn bottom ash into "green aggregates",

298
00:19:51,308 --> 00:19:54,200
a technology since licensed to industry.

299
00:19:54,200 --> 00:19:57,763
And under the same programme,
a Nanyang Technological

300
00:19:57,843 --> 00:20:01,406
University team spent three
years, from 2021 to 2024,

301
00:20:01,486 --> 00:20:05,392
on a project with the splendid
name "Breaking the Ground":

302
00:20:05,472 --> 00:20:09,516
a study of what, exactly, is
inside Semakau's filled cells —

303
00:20:09,596 --> 00:20:13,502
some fifteen million tonnes
of mixed material in the first

304
00:20:13,582 --> 00:20:17,500
phase alone — and whether it
can be dug back up and used.

305
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:20,000
The study is complete.

306
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,275
No excavation has been announced.

307
00:20:23,275 --> 00:20:27,877
But the question has been asked,
with money and engineers behind it,

308
00:20:27,957 --> 00:20:32,215
and it changes what the landfill
is: not a final resting place,

309
00:20:32,295 --> 00:20:35,325
necessarily, but a
stockpile awaiting a use.

310
00:20:35,325 --> 00:20:39,475
The island is already a
laboratory in another way.

311
00:20:39,475 --> 00:20:43,395
Since 2014 it has hosted REIDS,
a renewable-energy testbed

312
00:20:43,475 --> 00:20:47,050
run by the university — a
small, working model of the

313
00:20:47,130 --> 00:20:50,844
microgrids that Southeast
Asia's off-grid islands need,

314
00:20:50,924 --> 00:20:55,327
mixing solar panels, Singapore's
largest wind turbine, batteries,

315
00:20:55,407 --> 00:20:59,327
diesel and a hydrogen system,
and supplying the landfill's

316
00:20:59,407 --> 00:21:03,200
own facilities with a few
hundred kilowatt-hours a day.

317
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:06,558
A rubbish island that
generates its own power,

318
00:21:06,638 --> 00:21:10,600
monitors its own mangroves,
and is studied as a mine.

319
00:21:10,600 --> 00:21:14,227
In June 2026 the newest
institution arrived: TREASURES,

320
00:21:14,307 --> 00:21:17,933
a national research centre
for residue and toxic waste,

321
00:21:18,013 --> 00:21:22,920
funded with 35 million Singapore
dollars, set up by the environment agency

322
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,694
and the university, with a
whole division devoted to the

323
00:21:26,774 --> 00:21:31,344
landfill's transformation — recovering,
treating and repurposing what

324
00:21:31,424 --> 00:21:35,400
has been sent to Semakau, and
what is already stored there.

325
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:39,885
Its co-director, Professor Chu
Jian, put the ambition plainly:

326
00:21:39,965 --> 00:21:44,302
to turn Semakau from a disposal
site into a "transit storage

327
00:21:44,382 --> 00:21:48,800
and processing hub", and perhaps
extend its life by decades.

328
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:52,236
Nothing has been built
yet; the centre's first

329
00:21:52,316 --> 00:21:55,450
grants were called in the middle of 2026.

330
00:21:55,450 --> 00:21:58,631
But the direction of
travel is set, and it runs

331
00:21:58,711 --> 00:22:01,823
opposite to the barges:
for twenty-seven years

332
00:22:01,903 --> 00:22:05,025
the island has taken
what Singapore discards.

333
00:22:05,025 --> 00:22:09,300
The plan, now, is for it to
start giving some of it back.

334
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:13,000
The island's people, meanwhile,
have been rebuilding their

335
00:22:13,080 --> 00:22:16,600
community where they can —
which is not on the island.

336
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:21,853
In 2020 Firdaus Sani, a
fourth-generation Orang Laut descendant,

337
00:22:21,933 --> 00:22:25,850
began a page of family
photographs and stories.

338
00:22:25,850 --> 00:22:30,632
His maternal grandparents had
lived on Semakau until 1977,

339
00:22:30,712 --> 00:22:33,902
and the family, moved to Telok Blangah,

340
00:22:33,982 --> 00:22:38,425
had chosen the estate partly
to stay near open water.

341
00:22:38,425 --> 00:22:43,502
The page became Orang Laut SG, a
community organisation running research,

342
00:22:43,582 --> 00:22:46,610
education, coastal tours
and communal feasts

343
00:22:46,690 --> 00:22:50,707
cooked from his grandmother's
recipes — the food, he says,

344
00:22:50,787 --> 00:22:55,025
is a way of staying connected
to where the family came from.

345
00:22:55,025 --> 00:22:59,989
In June 2025 the organisation
held the first Hari Orang Pulau,

346
00:23:00,069 --> 00:23:02,837
Islanders' Day, at West Coast Park,

347
00:23:02,917 --> 00:23:08,775
reuniting the families of islands
most Singaporeans have never heard of.

348
00:23:08,775 --> 00:23:12,723
The carnival the southern
islands once took turns hosting,

349
00:23:12,803 --> 00:23:18,150
with its swimming races and greasy pole,
has a mainland descendant after all.

350
00:23:18,150 --> 00:23:21,992
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has closed many

351
00:23:22,072 --> 00:23:26,306
worlds to build its present — the
river trade, the attap villages,

352
00:23:26,386 --> 00:23:29,902
the mainland dumps — but
Semakau is the rare case where

353
00:23:29,982 --> 00:23:34,100
the replacement is still visibly
arguing with what it replaced.

354
00:23:34,100 --> 00:23:38,525
A landfill that had to rescue
a reef from its own lagoon.

355
00:23:38,525 --> 00:23:42,028
A village island underneath
the receiving station,

356
00:23:42,108 --> 00:23:44,975
whose shopkeepers came back as tourists.

357
00:23:44,975 --> 00:23:49,125
An island of ash that the
country now hopes to mine.

358
00:23:49,125 --> 00:23:53,676
Minah and Rani, who gave away their
flat and went home to a beach hut,

359
00:23:53,756 --> 00:23:57,844
were early with the island's
essential fact, the one every plan

360
00:23:57,924 --> 00:24:02,225
since has had to reckon with:
people were attached to this place.

361
00:24:02,225 --> 00:24:05,125
"Our hearts are attached to the island."

362
00:24:05,125 --> 00:24:07,475
The rubbish came later.

363
00:24:07,475 --> 00:24:10,025
The attachment, in one form or another,

364
00:24:10,105 --> 00:24:13,599
is still there — and so,
now, is a research programme

365
00:24:13,679 --> 00:24:17,050
betting that even the
rubbish is not finished yet.
